rlm-git-add-signer <github-username> [<email>]

Trust a GitHub user's SSH signing keys for git commit signature
verification. Fetches every signing key from
https://api.github.com/users/<github-username>/ssh_signing_keys and
appends it to git's gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile, once per principal,
skipping any (principal, key) pair already in the file.

The signing-keys API is used deliberately, not
https://github.com/<github-username>.keys: that URL serves only
authentication keys, a different set that does not sign commits.
GitHub's own "Verified" badge checks the account's signing-key list,
so this brings the local allowed_signers file in line with it.

The API returns no email, so two principals are trusted per key:
<github-username>@users.noreply.github.com and, when passed as the
second argument or entered at the prompt, the user's real commit
email. Each appended entry carries the namespaces="git" option and is
preceded by a full-line comment (the allowed_signers format has no
trailing-comment field) recording the date, the GitHub user, the
key's GitHub title and its fingerprint. Existing entries are never
rewritten or removed.

The target file is read from `git config gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile`,
falling back to ~/.config/git/allowed_signers; it and its parent
directory are created if missing.

ARGUMENTS
  <github-username>   GitHub account whose signing keys to fetch (required).
  <email>            Real commit email to also trust (optional; prompted
                     for when omitted, Enter skips it).

REQUIRES
  git, curl, jq. ssh-keygen is used when present to record key
  fingerprints in the comment lines.
